=== layout: section

# Humans and BioTech

=== layout: subsection

# 21. Assisted Reproductive Technology (ART)

=== layout: bookend

# 21. Questions

@body

- Historical perspective: Who was the first "test tube baby"? When was she born? Explain the controversy of IVF at the time. Who is Doris Del-Zio?
- Impact of IVF: How common is in vitro fertilization (IVF) today? How has IVF changed over the years?
- Where does fertilization occur in IUI and IVF? Which one counts as ART in U.S. reporting?
- Modern Innovations: What does each person contribute in mitochondrial donation? What are the controversies around preimplantation genetic diagnosis? How predictable would eye color be?
- Freezing Eggs: What are the main reasons individuals choose to freeze their eggs? Does oocyte cryopreservation work?

@citation

Discussion prompt source: BIOL 480, Fall 2026 Set #7, p. 57.

=== layout: one-panel

# 21. Follow the Egg, Sperm, and Embryo

@body

- In IVF, fertilization takes place outside the body. An embryo may later be transferred into a uterus.
- In IUI, sperm are placed in the uterus. Fertilization, if it occurs, takes place inside the body.
- Louise Brown, the first child born through IVF, was born in England in 1978.

@citation

[CDC, About assisted reproductive technology](https://www.cdc.gov/art/about/index.html)  |  [Nobel Assembly, Robert Edwards and IVF (2010)](https://www.nobelprize.org/uploads/2018/06/popular-medicineprize2010.pdf)

=== layout: one-panel

# 21. Doris Del-Zio and a Disputed Experiment

@body

- Doris and John Del-Zio attempted IVF in the United States in 1973.
- A physician at the hospital stopped the experiment by removing the culture from the incubator.
- The case asks how experimental oversight and the wishes of the patient should interact.

@citation

[Library of Congress, Del Zio v. Presbyterian Hospital](https://www.loc.gov/exhibitions/drawing-justice-courtroom-illustrations/about-this-exhibition/significant-and-landmark-cases/frozen-embryos-as-personal-property/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 21. Who Contributes to an IVF Pregnancy?

@body

- An egg donor and sperm donor contribute genetic material.
- A gestational carrier can carry an embryo formed using an egg from another person.
- Distinguish genetic contribution, pregnancy, and the role of raising a child.

@citation

[HFEA, Mitochondrial donation treatment](https://www.hfea.gov.uk/treatments/embryo-testing-and-treatments-for-disease/mitochondrial-donation-treatment)  |  [CDC, About assisted reproductive technology](https://www.cdc.gov/art/about/index.html)

=== layout: two-panels

# 21. Why Does the Location of Fertilization Matter?

@left

*IUI*

Sperm enter the uterus. The egg stays inside the body. U.S. ART surveillance excludes IUI alone.

@right

*IVF*

Eggs are collected and can be fertilized in the lab. U.S. ART surveillance includes treatments that handle eggs or embryos.

@citation

[CDC, About assisted reproductive technology](https://www.cdc.gov/art/about/index.html)  |  [Nobel Assembly, Robert Edwards and IVF (2010)](https://www.nobelprize.org/uploads/2018/06/popular-medicineprize2010.pdf)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Follow the Egg](assets/set7_drawings/fertilization_site.drawn.svg)

@caption

Topic 21: Follow the Egg\
Where is the egg when fertilization takes place in each treatment?

@citation

[CDC, About assisted reproductive technology](https://www.cdc.gov/art/about/index.html)  |  [Nobel Assembly, Robert Edwards and IVF (2010)](https://www.nobelprize.org/uploads/2018/06/popular-medicineprize2010.pdf)

=== layout: one-panel

# 21. Whose Mitochondria?

@body

Mitochondrial donation can help a family at risk of passing on serious mitochondrial disease.

- The child has nuclear DNA from the egg and sperm providers and mitochondrial DNA mostly from the mitochondrial donor.
- "Three parents" hides how different those contributions are. It also leaves out who carries and raises the child.

@citation

[HFEA, Mitochondrial donation treatment](https://www.hfea.gov.uk/treatments/embryo-testing-and-treatments-for-disease/mitochondrial-donation-treatment)

=== layout: one-panel

# 21. A Frozen Egg Is One Chance

@body

Egg freezing preserves eggs at the age when they were collected. Some eggs may fail to survive warming, fertilize or develop into an embryo.

- Imagine two clinics advertise the same number of births. One reports births per embryo transfer. The other reports births per patient who froze eggs.
- Which number answers a patient's question before any eggs have been collected?

@citation

[HFEA, Egg freezing in fertility treatment](https://www.hfea.gov.uk/media/2658/egg-freezing-in-fertility-treatment-supplement.pdf)

=== layout: one-panel

# 21. How Many Babies Were Born Through IVF?

@body

U.S. clinics reporting to SART recorded *100,158 babies* in their 2024 IVF data.

- *97,549 treatment cycles* resulted in at least one live birth. Why can there be more babies than successful cycles?
- A twin delivery counts as one cycle with a live birth and two babies. What denominator would a patient need to ask about success per cycle started?

@citation

[ASRM and SART, 2024 IVF outcomes (released March 2026)](https://www.asrm.org/news-and-events/asrm-news/press-releasesbulletins/for-the-first-time-more-than-100000-babies-born-through-ivf-in-the-u.s.-in-a-single-year/)

=== layout: subsection

# 22. Prenatal Testing

=== layout: bookend

# 22. Questions

@body

- What is prenatal genetic screening?
- What does a positive screening result mean?
- How can a rare condition affect the chance that a positive result is correct?

@citation

Discussion prompt source: BIOL 480, Fall 2026 Set #7, p. 60.

=== layout: one-panel

# 22. Screening Estimates Risk

@body

- Cell-free DNA screening uses fragments in a pregnant patient's blood. The pregnancy-related fraction comes mainly from the placenta.
- A positive screen means the chance is higher. Diagnostic testing can address whether the condition is present.
- The chance a positive result is correct depends partly on how common the condition is.

@citation

[MedlinePlus, Prenatal cell-free DNA screening](https://medlineplus.gov/lab-tests/prenatal-cell-free-dna-screening/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 22. Work the Numbers Before Interpreting the Result

@body

A hypothetical screen flags 20 of 1,000 pregnancies.

| Result | Condition present | Condition absent |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Positive screen | 10 | 10 |
| Negative screen | 0 | 980 |

*10 of 20 positive results are correct in this example.*

@citation

[MedlinePlus, Prenatal cell-free DNA screening](https://medlineplus.gov/lab-tests/prenatal-cell-free-dna-screening/)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Keep Only the Positive Screens](assets/set7_drawings/positive_screens.drawn.svg)

@caption

Topic 22: Count the Positive Screens\
Of these 20 pregnancies, how many have the condition?

@citation

[MedlinePlus, Prenatal cell-free DNA screening](https://medlineplus.gov/lab-tests/prenatal-cell-free-dna-screening/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 22. Now Make the Condition Rarer

@body

Use the same imaginary screen as before. Assume it detects every affected pregnancy and flags about 1% of unaffected pregnancies.

- In 10,000 pregnancies, suppose only 10 have the condition. Expect 10 true positives and about 100 false positives.
- Of about 110 positive results, roughly 9% are true positives. What changed from the earlier 50% example?

@citation

[MedlinePlus, Prenatal cell-free DNA screening](https://medlineplus.gov/lab-tests/prenatal-cell-free-dna-screening/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 22. A Positive Screen and a Different Diagnostic Result

@body

Most pregnancy-related cell-free DNA in maternal blood comes from the placenta. Occasionally some placental cells have a chromosome change that fetal cells do not share.

- A screen can pick up a real placental signal.
- Genetic counseling and appropriate diagnostic testing help a patient learn what that signal means for this pregnancy.

@citation

[Choi et al., Confined placental mosaicism (2013)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23192749/)  |  [MedlinePlus, Prenatal cell-free DNA screening](https://medlineplus.gov/lab-tests/prenatal-cell-free-dna-screening/)

=== layout: subsection

# 23. He Jiankui and Genome Editing in Humans

=== layout: bookend

# 23. Questions

@body

- What ethical concerns arise from the potential use of CRISPR to create designer babies?
- What are the ethical implications of making heritable changes to the human genome?

@citation

Discussion prompt source: BIOL 480, Fall 2026 Set #7, p. 62.

=== layout: one-panel

# 23. The 2018 Announcement

@body

- He Jiankui announced the birth of children from embryos whose genomes had been edited.
- The intended goal was to reduce susceptibility to HIV. The intervention raised questions about medical need, consent, and safety.
- A heritable edit can affect future generations.

@citation

[National Academies, Second International Summit (2018)](https://www.nationalacademies.org/read/25343)  |  [WHO, Human genome editing recommendations (2021)](https://www.who.int/publications-detail-redirect/9789240030381)

=== layout: one-panel

# 23. Who Can Consent to a Heritable Change?

@body

- In 2023, FDA approved Casgevy for eligible patients with sickle cell disease. It uses the patient's own edited blood stem cells.
- Embryo editing can expose the future child and later generations to uncertainty.
- Ask what alternatives existed and who had the authority to approve the work.

@citation

[International Commission, Heritable Human Genome Editing (2020)](https://nap.nationalacademies.org/resource/25665/Heritable%20Human%20Genome%20Editing%20Report%20Summary%20-%20FINAL%2020200903.pdf)  |  [FDA, Casgevy approval for sickle cell disease (2023)](https://www.fda.gov/news-events/press-announcements/fda-approves-first-gene-therapies-treat-patients-sickle-cell-disease)

=== layout: one-panel

# 23. What Evidence Did the Announcement Leave Out?

@body

The 2018 summit organizers heard the claim that children had been born after embryo editing. They challenged the medical justification, study design, and protection of participants.

- A birth alone cannot show that the intended health benefit occurred.
- What responsibility do investigators have to protect the children's privacy during follow-up?

@citation

[National Academies, Second International Summit (2018)](https://www.nationalacademies.org/read/25343)

=== layout: one-panel

# 23. Who Lives with the Decision?

@body

Compare a consenting adult considering a change to blood-forming cells with a proposed edit to an embryo intended for pregnancy.

- In the first case, follow-up centers on the person receiving treatment.
- In the second, the future child would live with the outcome. A change in cells that make eggs or sperm could also reach descendants.

@citation

[International Commission, Heritable Human Genome Editing (2020)](https://nap.nationalacademies.org/resource/25665/Heritable%20Human%20Genome%20Editing%20Report%20Summary%20-%20FINAL%2020200903.pdf)

=== layout: subsection

# 24. Directed Human Evolution (GATTACA)

=== layout: bookend

# 24. Questions

@body

- What is the difference between therapeutic editing and enhancement editing in human embryos?
- How does preimplantation genetic testing already guide reproductive decisions today?
- What traits might parents choose if trait selection became routine?
- What social risks arise if genetic enhancement becomes available only to wealthy families?
- How might directed evolution alter concepts of fairness, diversity, and disability?

@citation

Discussion prompt source: BIOL 480, Fall 2026 Set #7, p. 64.

=== layout: one-panel

# 24. Selection, Treatment, and Enhancement

@body

- Preimplantation genetic testing can inform a choice among embryos. Editing changes DNA.
- Treating a serious disease and choosing a preferred trait raise different questions.
- Many traits reflect many genetic variants plus the environment.

@citation

[HFEA, Embryo testing for inherited conditions](https://www.hfea.gov.uk/treatments/embryo-testing-and-treatments-for-disease/pre-implantation-genetic-testing-for-monogenic-disorders-pgt-m-and-pre-implantation-genetic-testing-for-chromosomal-structural-rearrangements-pgt-sr)  |  [Martin et al., Polygenic risk scores and health disparities (2019)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30926966/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 24. GATTACA and a Prediction Used as a Rule

@body

- In the film, genetic predictions affect a person's opportunities.
- A prediction is a probability. Its accuracy may vary across populations.
- Who would be disadvantaged if employers treated probabilities as certainties?

@citation

[Sony Pictures, Gattaca (1997)](https://www.sonypictures.com/movies/gattaca)  |  [Martin et al., Polygenic risk scores and health disparities (2019)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30926966/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 24. When the Family Variant Is Known

@body

One parent carries a particular disease-causing variant. During IVF, a clinic can test embryos for that familial variant.

- The result can inform which embryo to transfer. The test changes the information available to the family.
- What options remain if no embryo meets the family's criteria?

@citation

[HFEA, Embryo testing for inherited conditions](https://www.hfea.gov.uk/treatments/embryo-testing-and-treatments-for-disease/pre-implantation-genetic-testing-for-monogenic-disorders-pgt-m-and-pre-implantation-genetic-testing-for-chromosomal-structural-rearrangements-pgt-sr)

=== layout: two-panels

# 24. Could an Employer Screen Vincent?

@left

*In GATTACA*

Vincent loses access to jobs because of his genetic profile.

@right

*In the United States*

GINA limits how covered employers and health insurers use genetic information. Life, disability, and long-term-care insurance fall outside this law.

@citation

[NHGRI, Genetic discrimination](https://www.genome.gov/about-genomics/policy-issues/Genetic-Discrimination)  |  [Sony Pictures, Gattaca (1997)](https://www.sonypictures.com/movies/gattaca)

=== layout: one-panel

# 24. A Risk Score Travels to Another Population

@body

A score built from one ancestry group may predict disease less accurately in people whose ancestry was poorly represented in the training data.

- Before using the score in a new population, compare predictions with observed outcomes there.
- If opportunities depend on the score, the cost of prediction error may fall unevenly.

@citation

[Martin et al., Polygenic risk scores and health disparities (2019)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30926966/)

=== layout: subsection

# 25. Synthetic Wombs and Ectogenesis

=== layout: bookend

# 25. Questions

@body

- Which part of gestation has been supported outside the body in animals? What would a human study still need to show?
- How might partial support change care for extreme prematurity?
- Which parts of fetal development are easiest or hardest to support outside the body?
- How might ectogenesis shift parental roles, legal definitions of pregnancy, or reproductive rights?
- What long-term outcomes would researchers need to track in a future human study?
- How would society respond if ectogenesis became safer than traditional pregnancy?

@citation

Discussion prompt source: BIOL 480, Fall 2026 Set #7, p. 66.

=== layout: one-panel

# 25. Support During Part of Development

@body

- In a 2017 study, fetal lambs continued developing in a fluid-filled system for up to four weeks.
- The system modeled aspects of the womb during part of gestation.
- Support for premature development and an entire pregnancy outside the body are separate claims.

@citation

[Partridge et al., Extra-uterine support in lambs (2017)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28440792/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 25. What Would a Human Study Need to Establish?

@body

- Compare survival and long-term development with existing neonatal care.
- Ask how parents could make a decision under stress and uncertainty.
- Decide which harms would prompt researchers to stop a study.

@citation

[Partridge et al., Extra-uterine support in lambs (2017)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28440792/)  |  [FDA, Step 3 in drug development: Clinical research](https://www.fda.gov/patients/drug-development-process/step-3-clinical-research)

=== layout: one-panel

# 25. What Did the Lamb Study Measure?

@body

The team supported fetal lambs for up to four weeks. The lungs continued to mature and the animals grew during support.

- The lambs had already developed inside a ewe before entering the system.
- An experiment that starts late in gestation cannot answer whether the same system could support an embryo from fertilization.

@citation

[Partridge et al., Extra-uterine support in lambs (2017)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28440792/)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Which Part of Gestation Was Studied?](assets/set7_drawings/lamb_support.drawn.svg)

@caption

Topic 25: Which Part of Gestation Was Studied?\
Find the transfer point. What had already happened?

@citation

[Partridge et al., Extra-uterine support in lambs (2017)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28440792/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 25. Which Comparison Would Matter to a Family?

@body

Imagine a future study offers extra-uterine support for extreme prematurity.

- Compare outcomes with the neonatal care available at the same developmental stage.
- Counting days of support tells families something different from counting survivors with long-term follow-up.

@citation

[Partridge et al., Extra-uterine support in lambs (2017)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28440792/)  |  [FDA, Step 3 in drug development: Clinical research](https://www.fda.gov/patients/drug-development-process/step-3-clinical-research)

=== layout: subsection

# 26. Ethical Sourcing of Biological Materials

=== layout: bookend

# 26. Questions

@body

- How important is informed consent in the collection of human tissues for research? Can you provide an example where consent was not properly obtained?
- Who was Henrietta Lacks, and why is her story significant in the context of ethical sourcing of biological materials?
- What is biopiracy? How does biopiracy using patents affect the livelihoods and cultural heritage of indigenous and local communities? Can you analyze a well-known case of biopiracy, discussing both the biological material involved and the community affected? What ethical guidelines should biotechnology companies follow to avoid biopiracy?

@citation

Discussion prompt source: BIOL 480, Fall 2026 Set #7, p. 68.

=== layout: one-panel

# 26. HeLa and Consent

@body

- In 1951, cells taken from Henrietta Lacks's cervical tumor became a widely used research cell line.
- She did not give permission for the research use of those cells.
- Her case connects consent, privacy, and who benefits from research.

@citation

[NIH, The NIH-Lacks Family Agreement](https://grants.nih.gov/policy-and-compliance/policy-topics/sharing-policies/dms/privacy/nih-lacks-family-agreement)

=== layout: one-panel

# 26. Who Supplied the Material and the Knowledge?

@body

- A resource may come from a community that has maintained it or shared knowledge about it.
- Biopiracy concerns include taking that resource or knowledge without permission or fair benefit sharing.
- Ask who can give permission and what the agreement should cover.

@citation

[Convention on Biological Diversity, Nagoya Protocol, Article 5](https://www.cbd.int/abs/text/articles?sec=abs-05)

=== layout: one-panel

# 26. Neem Oil and Prior Knowledge

@body

- A European patent claimed a way to control plant fungi using neem oil.
- The European Patent Office considered prior knowledge and upheld the revocation.
- A patent decision addresses specific claims. Fair credit and benefit sharing are additional ethical questions.

@citation

[European Patent Office, Decision T 0416/01 (2005)](https://www.epo.org/en/boards-of-appeal/decisions/t010416eu1)

=== layout: one-panel

# 26. Why DNA Data Can Affect Relatives

@body

A research team wants to post a HeLa genome sequence. Henrietta Lacks's relatives share some inherited variants with her.

- In 2013, NIH and the family agreed on controlled access to certain HeLa genome data. Family representatives take part in access review.
- Growing cells and publishing identifiable family information raise different permission questions.

@citation

[NIH, The NIH-Lacks Family Agreement](https://grants.nih.gov/policy-and-compliance/policy-topics/sharing-policies/dms/privacy/nih-lacks-family-agreement)

=== layout: one-panel

# 26. A Patent Office and a Community Answer Different Questions

@body

The neem patent appeal examined earlier public use of the claimed method. A patent can fail if that method was already known.

- Revoking a claim does not set the price of access or decide who shares future benefits.
- Benefit-sharing agreements can address training, credit, or payment. Which would matter to the community in your example?

@citation

[Convention on Biological Diversity, Nagoya Protocol, Article 5](https://www.cbd.int/abs/text/articles?sec=abs-05)  |  [European Patent Office, Decision T 0416/01 (2005)](https://www.epo.org/en/boards-of-appeal/decisions/t010416eu1)

=== layout: subsection

# 27. Embryonic Stem Cells

=== layout: bookend

# 27. Questions

@body

- Are there viable alternatives to using embryonic stem cells? Is a human embryo equivalent to a human child? Is the destruction of a single embryo justified if it provides a cure for millions of patients? Can federal funds be used to research embryonic stem cells? Who is Haruko Obokata? Is "stimulus-triggered acquisition of pluripotency" or STAP even possible today?

@citation

Discussion prompt source: BIOL 480, Fall 2026 Set #7, p. 70.

=== layout: one-panel

# 27. Different Sources of Pluripotent Cells

@body

- Embryonic stem cells come from early embryos and can form many cell types.
- Induced pluripotent stem cells are produced by reprogramming already specialized cells.
- Both are research tools. A useful cell in culture is an early step toward a possible treatment.

@citation

[Thomson et al., Human embryonic stem cell lines (1998)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9804556/)  |  [Takahashi et al., Human induced pluripotent stem cells (2007)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18035408/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 27. A Claim About Stemness Can Be Tested

@body

- The STAP reports claimed that stress could turn specialized cells into pluripotent cells.
- The reports were retracted after serious problems with the work.
- A claim about cell identity needs convincing markers, functional evidence, and independent replication.

@citation

[Nature, Retraction of the STAP paper (2014)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24990753/)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Haruko Obokata and the STAP Claims](assets/set7_instructor/haruko_obokata.jpg)

@caption

Topic 27: Haruko Obokata and the STAP Claims\
The retracted reports make this a case about reproducibility as well as cell identity.

@citation

Course image: instructor ethics deck, p. 55.

=== layout: one-panel

# 27. Two Starting Materials, One Property to Test

@body

One group derived cell lines from early human embryos. Another began with adult human skin cells and reprogrammed them.

- Both groups needed evidence that the cells could develop into many kinds of specialized cells.
- An iPS cell offers a way to study a patient's own genetic background. Which part of the embryo-sourcing debate changes?

@citation

[Thomson et al., Human embryonic stem cell lines (1998)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9804556/)  |  [Takahashi et al., Human induced pluripotent stem cells (2007)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18035408/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 27. A Headline After a Retraction

@body

Imagine an article says, "Stress turns ordinary cells into stem cells," and links to the retracted STAP report.

- Follow the paper to the journal notice. The authors withdrew the report because they could no longer stand by it.
- A fair headline would describe the withdrawal. Pointing to independent iPS cell results would not validate the STAP claim.

@citation

[Nature, Retraction of the STAP paper (2014)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24990753/)  |  [COPE, Retraction guidelines](https://doi.org/10.24318/cope.2019.1.4)

=== layout: one-panel

# 27. Can NIH Fund Work on Embryonic Stem Cells?

@body

The 2009 NIH guidelines set conditions for using eligible human embryonic stem cell lines in NIH-funded research. They distinguish research using an existing line from deriving the line.

- Among the conditions for certain IVF-derived lines are voluntary donation, informed consent, and embryos no longer needed for reproduction.
- To answer a funding question, first identify the cell line and the activity the grant would support.

@citation

[NIH, 2009 Guidelines for Human Stem Cell Research](https://stemcells.nih.gov/research-policy/guidelines-for-human-stem-cell-research)

=== layout: subsection

# 28. Biohackers and Josette Zayner

=== layout: bookend

# 28. Questions

@body

- What are the potential benefits of citizen science in biology?
- What are the risks when a public demonstration involves genetic self-experimentation?

@citation

Discussion prompt source: BIOL 480, Fall 2026 Set #7, p. 72.

=== layout: one-panel

# 28. Citizen Science and Self-experimentation

@body

- Community labs can make biology education and shared equipment more accessible.
- Josette Zayner, identified as Josiah in the original assignment, conducted a public self-experiment in 2017.
- A demonstration can show that someone tried something. It cannot establish a reliable clinical result.

@citation

[Oral History Project, Interview with Zayner](https://protocol.ecologies.info/interviews/zayner-biohacking/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 28. Who Else Can Be Affected?

@body

- A public demonstration can encourage imitation.
- Ask how bystanders, volunteers, or future patients might be affected.
- Compare a supervised education project with an untested intervention in a person.

@citation

[FDA, Self-administration of gene therapy](https://www.fda.gov/vaccines-blood-biologics/cellular-gene-therapy-products/information-about-self-administration-gene-therapy)

=== layout: one-panel

# 28. What Can a Public Demonstration Document?

@body

An audience saw Zayner take a personal risk. A recording documents that public act and what the speaker said.

- To establish a biological outcome, someone would need measurements made before and after, with a credible comparison.
- Which claim can a journalist verify from the video alone?

@citation

[Oral History Project, Interview with Zayner](https://protocol.ecologies.info/interviews/zayner-biohacking/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 28. When a Personal Story Becomes an Advertisement

@body

Imagine a seller links a personal demonstration directly to a product order page.

- A customer may read the story as a promise that the product works.
- Ask which benefit the page implies and whether the seller has evidence for that particular benefit.

@citation

[FDA, Self-administration of gene therapy](https://www.fda.gov/vaccines-blood-biologics/cellular-gene-therapy-products/information-about-self-administration-gene-therapy)  |  [FTC, Health Products Compliance Guidance](https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/health-products-compliance-guidance)

=== layout: subsection

# 29. Aaron Traywick and Rogue Biohackers

=== layout: bookend

# 29. Questions

@body

- What ethical problems arise when experiments occur without approval or medical supervision?
- How did Traywick differ from responsible community biology groups?

@citation

Discussion prompt source: BIOL 480, Fall 2026 Set #7, p. 74.

=== layout: one-panel

# 29. A Demonstration Is Weak Evidence

@body

- Aaron Traywick promoted experimental treatments outside normal clinical oversight.
- An uncontrolled demonstration cannot show whether an intervention works or whether harms are common.
- A credible study defines outcomes, monitors harms, and states what would stop the study.

@citation

[Sarah Zhang, Interview with Zayner on the Traywick demonstration (2018)](https://www.theatlantic.com/science/archive/2018/02/biohacking-stunts-crispr/553511/)  |  [FDA, Step 3 in drug development: Clinical research](https://www.fda.gov/patients/drug-development-process/step-3-clinical-research)

=== layout: one-panel

# 29. Consent Under Uncertainty

@body

- A volunteer needs to understand the evidence, risks, and alternatives.
- Publicity and promises of a cure can put pressure on the decision.
- What would independent review add?

@citation

[The Belmont Report (1979)](https://www.hhs.gov/ohrp/regulations-and-policy/belmont-report/read-the-belmont-report/index.html)

=== layout: one-panel

# 29. An Audience Cannot Supply a Comparison Group

@body

Traywick publicly promoted an experimental intervention. Suppose a volunteer says afterward that symptoms improved.

- Symptoms can change over time, with or without an intervention.
- A study must compare outcomes and look for harms, including outcomes that volunteers never post online.

@citation

[Sarah Zhang, Interview with Zayner on the Traywick demonstration (2018)](https://www.theatlantic.com/science/archive/2018/02/biohacking-stunts-crispr/553511/)  |  [FDA, Step 3 in drug development: Clinical research](https://www.fda.gov/patients/drug-development-process/step-3-clinical-research)

=== layout: one-panel

# 29. Who Hears About a Bad Outcome?

@body

In a hypothetical demonstration, the promoter is also the only person collecting feedback.

- A volunteer with a bad outcome may worry about disappointing the group.
- A private route for reporting harm and a reviewer independent of the promoter can change what becomes visible.

@citation

